{:edb/db-before-t 1267 :edb/db-after-t 1268 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534580 50 #inst "2026-10-12T03:40:57.099Z" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166274 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166277 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166670 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166670 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186166671 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166671 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186166672 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in \\{9,10\\}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166672 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in {9,10}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426430 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426432 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426433 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426434 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426437 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426425 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426426 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426427 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426431 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426429 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426436 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426439 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426428 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426435 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426438 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186167043 1050 1020.0 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186167511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186167511 1071 "Given that $X∼B(5, 0.6)$, compute $P(X > 1)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186167511 1071 "Given that $X∼B(5,0.6),$ compute $P(X>1).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186167511 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>1)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,4,5.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 1)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{5-1} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{5}+5(0.6)(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0870\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =1-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0870 \\\\  & =0.9130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186168130 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of heads obtained when a coin is tossed $\\color{blue}3$ times. Since we have a sequence of $n=\\color{blue}3$ Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac12,$ the number of heads $X$ can be modeled as a binomial random variable, and we write \n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left({\\color{blue}3}, \\dfrac12\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting $X=2$ heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3\\,-\\,2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186168130 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of heads obtained when a coin is tossed $\\color{blue}3$ times. Since we have a sequence of $n=\\color{blue}3$ Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac12,$ the number of heads $X$ can be modeled as a binomial random variable, and we write \n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left({\\color{blue}3}, \\dfrac12\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting $X=2$ heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3\\,-\\,2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186265794 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186265794 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186295112 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186295112 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 false] [17592186360826 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{3-3} \\\\  & =3(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186360826 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p