variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(0.5)}^{x}{(0.5)}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5}){(0.5)}^{5}{(0.5)}^{7-5} \\\\  & =21{(0.5)}^{5}{(0.5)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.164\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426413 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1069 "q-275955" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1071 "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}} ." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1072 #uuid "c4482a30-821e-5dc4-b28b-016916e46721" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426433 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.141\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426414 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1069 "q-279613" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1071 "If $X∼B(6,0.3),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1072 #uuid "d2704acc-87d3-5c87-a9ec-e5a36c5fdd82" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426434 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{6-1} \\\\  & =6(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.303\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426417 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1069 "q-129985" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1071 "Given that $X∼B(11,0.4),$ compute $P(X>1).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1072 #uuid "d3c970b3-ed82-5453-989f-95b7ad9d86a2" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426435 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(11,0.4),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{11-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>1)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 1)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{11-1} \\\\  & =1(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11}+11(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{10} \\\\  & ≈0.0302\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =1-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0302 \\\\  & =0.9698\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426423 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1069 "q-279701" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1071 "If $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{2}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(2\\le X\\le 3)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1072 #uuid "e0c29cf6-86b2-58fd-b05e-41adbe40c41c" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426436 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =15{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}+20{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.547\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426420 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1069 "q-129854" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1071 "Given that $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ compute $P(X=4).$" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1072 #uuid "e0fe361b-76eb-5e77-a52f-d24556387da9" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426437 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-4} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426415 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1069 "q-281206" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1071 "If $X∼B(11,0.65),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 10)≈${{field-1}}." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1072 #uuid "e5103b67-e208-5c70-9c50-ad3c3ab4c26a" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426438 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(11,0.65),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{x}){(0.65)}^{x}{(0.35)}^{11-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 10)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,…,10.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>10)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=11,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>10),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>10) & =P(X\\in \\{11\\}) \\\\  & =f(11) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{11}){(0.65)}^{11}{(0.35)}^{11-11} \\\\  & =1{(0.65)}^{11}{(0.35)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.009\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =1-P(X>10) \\\\  & ≈1-0.009 \\\\  & =0.991\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426421 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1069 "q-97517" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1071 "Given $X∼B(7,0.3),$ compute $P(6\\le X\\le 7).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1072 #uuid "f428e76f-4165-56e2-a17c-fa7e27edc2d5" 13194139534580 17592186045417 true] [17592186426439 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\le X\\le 7) & =P(X\\in \\{6,7\\}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.3)^{6}(0.7)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.3)^{6}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534580 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01db9526-8dcd-5f42-a095-1323e6d73cf3" 17592186426371 "answer-2868a9b8-174d-5afc-bb08-ca49c79aaf18" 17592186426372 "answer-2c29ad5a-6441-5af9-ae31-6bd58be51e3a" 17592186426373 "answer-4a99b9af-62d2-5730-ab23